{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794316845,"nonce":"UzRu+dYjd447JErho66eLo/h5ZM=","title":"wasm AOT: a recursive numeric function runs about 3x slower than the same code emitted with the same shadow-frame protocol","message":"ash --target wasm32-wasip1-threads --build of a Haxe fib(35), run with ash run on macOS (wasmtime): 168 ms. The same function emitted by the numeric prototype in crates/ash_wasm_emit with the same per-function shadow frame (push, position stores, pop) and run in the same wasmtime: 56 ms; with no frames at all, 26 ms. Loop polls cost LLVM's module about 4x on a tight loop (loopSum 200M iterations: 531 ms, 133 ms with ASH_FIBER_POLLS=0) against about 2.4x for a flag-test poll in the emitter (405 vs 170 ms).\n\nThe LLVM IR for fib (ASH_DUMP_FN_IR=258) shows per-function hlp_shadow_push/hlp_shadow_pop calls, a store of the position word into the frame at each block, and the integer constants loaded from globals (load i32, ptr @Int_9) instead of immediates. Which of these accounts for the gap is not established: std/src/error.rs's shadow::push is a counter and one 16-byte store. Worth checking the final wasm of fib for what remains after LLVM, and whether the polls' hlp_fiber_poll memory effects are what stops loop optimisation (ASH_POLL_MEMORY exists to test that).\n\nHarness: cargo test --release -p ash --test wasm_emit_numeric -- --ignored --nocapture (emitted side), crates/ash/test/tests/TestWasmBench.hx built with ash --build and run with ash run (LLVM side). Related: a1e1488.","files":null}]}